+# Decision: Delivery transport for committed board changes
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard keeps one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS and the server assigns each committed edit one event revision; that part of the boundary is settled. This decision is about the other end of the arrow: how a committed revision reaches the browsers already watching a board.
+
+The delivery transport shapes the rest of the design. The change store must retain whatever a reconnecting browser needs to catch up; the delivery node must own whatever publication mechanism the transport requires; and browser sync must implement whatever subscription and recovery behavior the transport imposes. Choosing the transport is therefore a choice about which downstream obligations the project takes on.
+
+All three transports considered can meet the required freshness bar — showing a committed change in a connected browser within fifteen seconds under normal connectivity. Freshness therefore does not eliminate any option; the decision turns on what each transport costs in operation, fees, and independence.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+### Weighted criteria
+
+- **Independence from a realtime vendor** — *major*. How much of the delivery path the project owns, and whether a third party sits on the critical path for board updates. This criterion engages the tenet *Self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted*: because managed infrastructure is permitted, independence is a preference this decision chooses to lean on, not a standing constraint that forbids a provider.
+- **Operating cost** — *major*. Whether delivery incurs recurring per-connection or per-message fees, versus infrastructure the project already runs.
+- **Operational burden** — *major*. Who owns connection capacity, session lifecycle, and reconnect behavior, and how much of that work lands on the project.
+- **Read load and delivery statelessness** — *moderate*. How much repeated work the transport generates against the board, and whether the delivery path holds state between updates.
+- **Update latency** — *minor*. How quickly a committed change becomes visible. Every option already clears the fifteen-second bar; lower latency is a capability margin, not a requirement.
+
+No gate eliminates an option: all three clear the freshness requirement, and no option crosses a declared budget or infrastructure boundary.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** Browsers poll the server over HTTPS on a fixed five-second interval, asking for revisions newer than the last one they hold. Delivery stays request/response and stateless: the server answers each poll from the change store and holds nothing between requests. The change store answers with cursor-style queries over the revision history, and browsers run the polling loop and apply returned revisions.
+
+**Demonstration.** A browser holds revision 418; its next poll asks for revisions after 418 and receives 419–421, which it applies before scheduling the next poll.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Independence — *strong*: no vendor participates; polling runs entirely on project-owned HTTPS infrastructure.
+- Operating cost — *strong*: no recurring fees; cost is the infrastructure the project already pays for.
+- Operational burden — *strong*: no long-lived connections, no session lifecycle, no reconnect logic to own.
+- Read load and statelessness — *weak*: every browser re-queries the board on every interval whether or not anything changed, so reads scale with browser count times poll rate rather than with actual change volume.
+- Update latency — *weak*: a change waits up to five seconds for the next poll, the slowest of the three, though still inside the required bar.
+
+**Summary.** The lowest-burden, lowest-cost option, paid for in repeated reads and up to five seconds of lag.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description.** The project runs its own WebSocket endpoint. A browser opens a connection and subscribes to an event's board; the server pushes each committed revision as it lands. Connections are long-lived, so the project owns connection capacity and the session lifecycle. A dropped connection is recovered by reconnecting and replaying revisions from the browser's last-seen revision, which requires the change store to keep revisions replayable.
+
+**Demonstration.** A browser subscribes to event 12 from revision 418; the server pushes 419, 420, and 421 as editors submit them. If the socket drops at 420, the reconnected browser asks for revisions after 420 and receives 421.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Independence — *strong*: the project owns the delivery path end to end; no third party sits on the update critical path.
+- Operating cost — *strong*: no recurring vendor fees; cost is infrastructure and the engineering to run it.
+- Operational burden — *weak*: the project owns connection capacity, the session lifecycle, and reconnect behavior, and must publish revisions reliably to every connected browser.
+- Read load and statelessness — *strong*: reads and pushes track actual change volume rather than browser count; delivery holds connection state, not repeated board reads.
+- Update latency — *strong*: revisions push on commit, well inside the required bar.
+
+**Summary.** Fast updates and full independence, paid for in connection and reconnect operations the project must own.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** A third-party realtime service carries the connections. The project publishes committed revisions to the provider, which fans them out to subscribed browsers. The delivery node wraps the provider and reconciles publication against it; browsers use the provider's own subscription model rather than a project-owned socket.
+
+**Demonstration.** A browser holds the provider's subscription for event 12; the project publishes revision 421 to the provider, which delivers it to every subscribed browser.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Independence — *weak*: the provider sits on the critical path for board updates, and browser behavior is bound to its subscription model.
+- Operating cost — *weak*: recurring per-connection or per-message fees scale with the 800-browser and opening-burst load.
+- Operational burden — *strong*: the provider carries connection capacity and reconnect handling, so the project runs fewer connection operations.
+- Read load and statelessness — *strong*: publication tracks actual change volume; the provider holds the connection state.
+- Update latency — *strong*: revisions reach browsers with low latency.
+
+**Summary.** The quickest route to low-latency updates and the least connection operations, paid for in recurring fees and provider coupling.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.** The decision trades operational burden for independence and cost. On the two major criteria it cannot satisfy at once, the project chooses independence and no recurring fees — WebSockets is *strong* on both — and accepts the *weak* operational burden of owning connection capacity and reconnect behavior. That weak point is the work the project has explicitly taken on.
+
+Polling loses because its advantage — the *strong* operational burden and statelessness it offers — buys lower burden at the cost of the *weak* read load and *weak* latency, without gaining independence or cost advantages over self-hosted WebSockets: it is the most read-heavy and slowest option while still running on the same project-owned infrastructure. The hosted service loses because it is *weak* on the two criteria the project weighted most heavily — independence and operating cost — in exchange for reducing an operational burden the project is willing to carry.
+
+The decision turns on the tenet *Self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted*. That tenet is what keeps the hosted service legitimately in contention; the project is choosing the self-hosted leaning on a preference, not excluding a provider that the project forbids. It also respects *A committed edit has one authoritative event revision regardless of its delivery route*: whichever transport carried a revision, the revision itself is the same, and this decision only chooses the route.
+
+Committing here obligates the downstream segments. The change store must retain replayable revisions so a reconnecting browser can catch up. The delivery node must own socket sessions, publish committed revisions reliably to connected browsers, and manage connection capacity. Browser sync must own connecting, reconnecting after a drop, and catching up from the last-seen revision. It forecloses the cursor-query polling model and the provider-subscription model as the primary delivery path.
