 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Delivery over self-hosted WebSockets.** Committed changes push to browsers over server-owned WebSocket connections. Five-second HTTPS polling and a hosted realtime service were both considered. Polling is stateless and operationally simplest but lags by up to five seconds and multiplies repeated reads against the board. A hosted service reaches low latency with the least connection operations but adds recurring fees and provider coupling, and requires publication reconciliation against the provider. Self-hosting gives fast updates without a realtime vendor; the project accepts owning connection capacity and reconnect behavior to get there. See `docs/decisions/delivery-transport.md`.
